Fellow sysadmins,

We have a couple of Tru64 UNIX V5.1B + PK3 clusters on a Gigabit =
network. They are clients to three mount points/filesystems being =
exported by a Solaris 2.6 system.

Output of a "mount" command from one of the clients-
sunnode:/sununix1/archive/PRD on =
/var/cluster/members/member2/opt/archive type nfs (v3, rw, udp, hard, =
intr, wsize=3D8192, rsize=3D8192)
sunnode:/sununix2/vault/PRD on /var/cluster/members/member2/opt/vault =
type nfs (v3, rw, udp, hard, intr, wsize=3D8192, rsize=3D8192)
sunnode:/sununix2/R3arch/P01 on /var/cluster/members/member2/opt/R3arch =
type nfs (v3, rw, udp, hard, intr, wsize=3D8192, rsize=3D8192)

We were mounting these filesystems on our Tru64 systems using tcp and =
not udp but the filesystems would hang. And we would have to reboot our =
clients to get them fixed back again. We moved to udp and it works well =
now. The fixed wsize & rsize are because the Tru64 clusters (NFS =
clients) are on a gigabit network and the Solaris system (NFS server) is =
on a 100Mbs network. Now the clients don't seem to hang but the response =
time is terrible. We tried to change the wsize/rsize to a higher and =
lower number but no dice...Has anyone seen such a problem across =
different speed networks? If so, how did you get around it?

The interesting thing is that there are other nodes (Tru64 UNIX clients) =
on the 100Mbs network that don't have this problem.

